  "brief_md": "## A18 — Resume Gap Reframing & Career Trajectory Tool\n\n**Idea:** A tool that reframes a resume with employment gaps and recommends a realistic next-career trajectory, for job seekers with career gaps.\n\n### Payer\nThe end job seeker pays directly (B2C). Price hypothesis: freemium model with paid tier in the $8–$24/month range, anchored to Kickresume's Online AI Career Coach pricing ($24/mo monthly, down to $8/mo annual) — https://www.kickresume.com/en/online-ai-career-coach/. A newer entrant, WeGoUp Coach, is pricing an equivalent Pro tier at $9.99/mo (https://wegoup.ai/).\n\n### Competitors\nAt least five products already do this or something adjacent:\n- **GapZero** (free, GDPR-compliant, deletes data post-analysis) — https://gapzero.app/\n- **Kickresume AI Career Coach** (free + $8–24/mo Premium) — https://www.kickresume.com/en/online-ai-career-coach/\n- **ResAlign AI** (free + paid plans, price unpublished) — https://resalign.com/\n- **WeGoUp Coach** (free + $9.99/mo Pro, currently pre-alpha) — https://wegoup.ai/\n- **CoachGPT Resume Gap Explainer** (pricing gated behind login, NOT FOUND) — https://coachgpt.com/tools/resume-gap-explainer\n\nThis is a crowded niche with functioning free alternatives already live.\n\n### Acquisition Channel\n- Google Search Ads in the \"Career & Employment\" category: cost-per-lead was $117.92 in 2024, falling ~46.7% YoY into 2025 (source: https://www.wordstream.com/blog/2024-google-ads-benchmarks, trend confirmed in https://www.wordstream.com/blog/2025-google-ads-benchmarks).\n- Facebook/Meta lead ads in the same category are comparatively cheap: ~$12.30 cost per lead (https://localiq.com/blog/facebook-advertising-benchmarks/).\nBoth suggest paid acquisition is workable but not cheap relative to a sub-$25/mo price tag; payback depends heavily on conversion rate, which is unverified.\n\n### Demand Evidence\nReal people describe acute anxiety and identity-related pain from resume gaps across forums:\n- \"There's a huge gap of 5 years in my CV... which could be a huge red flag\" — https://forums.redflagdeals.com/34-900k-net-worth-unemployed-more-than-1-year-how-deal-resume-gap-2592868/\n- \"How do I explain the 5-year gap in my resume due to mental health issues?\" — https://theindependent.sg/sg-woman-attending-a-job-interview-asks-how-do-i-explain-the-5-year-gap-in-my-resume-due-to-mental-health-issues/\n- \"I don't have $100–$150 per hour to spend on a career coach... but I do have a subscription to ChatGPT\" — https://every.to/working-overtime/i-hired-chatgpt-as-my-career-coach (illustrates willingness to substitute cheap AI tools for expensive human coaching)\n\n### Case For\nThe workflow (parse resume → identify gaps → benchmark against target role/salary → generate staged roadmap) is a bounded, mostly-text LLM pipeline buildable solo in six weeks with near-zero marginal cost. Multiple live competitors prove the feature set is buildable at small-team/solo scale and that users freely upload resumes to try such tools, offering a low-cost way to test a freemium paid tier at Kickresume/WeGoUp price points.\n\n### Case Against\nAt least four free-or-freemium direct competitors already exist, some using licensed government salary data (BLS/ONS/Eurostat) that raises the real build bar above a thin LLM wrapper. The need is largely one-time (ends at re-employment), capping LTV, while Career & Employment paid acquisition costs ($62–$118 CPL via Google) are high relative to an $8–$24/mo price. The subject matter often touches sensitive causes of gaps (health, caregiving, disability — evidenced in the demand quotes), creating real harm/reputational risk if a lightly-supervised AI gives poor 'trajectory' advice, in a customer segment that is often financially strained and price-sensitive.\n\n### Open Questions\n1. Will users pay specifically for gap-framing, or expect it bundled free into broader resume tools (as most competitors already do)?\n2. What free-to-paid conversion is realistically achievable for this narrow use case?\n3. Can a solo operator source credible salary/labor-market data without licensing issues, matching GapZero's BLS/ONS/Eurostat integration?\n4. Is there any retention/repeat-use mechanism, or is this fundamentally a one-and-done tool requiring continuous new-customer acquisition?\n5. Would a narrower add-on to an existing resume builder outperform a standalone paid product on unit economics — the evidence suggests this may be the case, but that would be a different idea than the one specified.\n\n### Regulatory Setup\nHandling uploaded resumes (and potentially sensitive personal data explaining gaps, e.g., health/caregiving) triggers GDPR/CCPA obligations. GapZero already advertises 'GDPR compliant, data deleted after analysis' as a competitive claim (https://gapzero.app/), setting a market expectation. One-time consult (~2–4 hrs) with a privacy attorney to draft privacy policy/ToS/data retention rules; ongoing obligation to actually implement deletion and non-resale practices."
